Upgrade Your Code Step by Step
******************************

.[perex]
How much work does it take to move to a newer Nette, Symfony, Laravel or PHP? DressCode measures it before it changes anything in your code: it tells you what it rewrites by itself, what waits until you raise the versions in `composer.json`, and what is left to you. Then it makes a plan and carries it out step by step, including raising the versions with Composer, and if a step breaks one of your tests, it reverts that step.

If you have not installed DressCode yet, [Getting Started |getting-started#Installation] shows how. The whole upgrade takes three commands: `dresscode upgrade estimate` measures, `upgrade plan` plans, and `upgrade run` carries the plan out. If you have already raised the versions yourself, [`upgrade today` |#When You Have Already Raised the Versions] is enough. Every option is described in the [command line reference |cli#upgrade].


How Much Work an Upgrade Takes
==============================

Take a Nette 3 application that is about to move to `nette/utils` 4.0; [below |#Where DressCode Runs] you will see how to install DressCode for it. All the estimate needs is a target: the package and the version you are moving to, or the word `php` and a PHP version:

```shell
dresscode upgrade estimate nette/utils 4.0
```

/--pre .[terminal]
DRESS|CODE 1.3.0
Config     /var/www/shop/dresscode.neon
Upgrade    nette/utils 4.0 over 3 files

Must change, 2 topics with 12 findings
  nette/utils  10 findings in 3 files    needs ^4.0, automatic
  nette/utils  2 findings in 1 file      works on ^3.2, automatic

Today      2 findings of `deprecations` are rewritten on the versions allowed now by `dresscode upgrade today`
Composer   nette/utils 4.0: nothing in the lock refuses it
Moves      nette/utils v3.2.10 => v4.0.0
Data       nette/application up to 3.3, nette/component-model up to 3.2, nette/http up to 3.4,
           nette/routing up to 3.1.4, nette/utils up to 4.1.5, php up to 8.6
\--

Each line is one topic: either a library DressCode has data about, here `nette/utils`, or a single [decision |configuration] for a change that no data describes. Next to the topic you see the number of findings (the places that must change), the number of files they are in, and two words that describe the topic.

The first word says when a place may be rewritten. `works on ^3.2` means that the new code already works on the versions `composer.json` allows today, `^3.2`, so the place may be rewritten right away. A place marked `needs ^4.0` waits until you raise the constraint to `^4.0`, because code written for the new version would not work on the old one. That is why one library can appear on both lines: Nette deprecated `Arrays::searchKey()` back in the 3.x series and its replacement `getKeyOffset()` already exists in 3.2, while `Strings::startsWith()` disappeared only in 4.0.

The second word says who rewrites the place. DressCode rewrites `automatic` places by itself. `manual` marks manual tasks: places where a person has to choose the replacement, with a message that says what to write. `unknown types` means the place waits for the types from PHPStan, and `uncertain names` that it waits until it is certain which function a call means.

The line `Today` counts what [`upgrade today` |#When You Have Already Raised the Versions] would rewrite right now. The line `Composer` says what in `composer.lock` refuses the target version: a package that requires an older `nette/utils` would be listed here with its constraint. The line `Moves` lists the packages that raising the constraint would move to a new major version, according to a dry run of Composer. A move the data does not cover is marked `(no data)`, and you make that step without the help of DressCode; a move the data covers only up to a lower minor version is marked `(data up to 4.1)`. The last line, `Data`, lists the libraries DressCode has data about, and the version the data reaches for each.

The estimate changes nothing. It asks only three questions: it asks Packagist which upgrade packages exist (at most once a day), Composer what in the lock blocks the target (no network needed), and Composer again what the raise would move (this one needs the network). With the option `--offline`, it skips the last question and uses only what it remembers from Packagist. A file the check cannot read, for example one that does not parse, is listed on the line `Unverified`, and its findings are not counted. An estimate that missed a file, lacked upgrade data or did not learn from Composer what blocks the target ends with exit code 2, so that nobody mistakes an incomplete number for a complete one. Without a target, it proposes the newest versions its data knows about, and `dresscode upgrade estimate php` does the same for PHP alone.

In a terminal, the estimate asks whether to use the types, because they tell which object each method is called on. `--types` turns them on without asking, and `--no-types` measures without them; every place that needs them is then marked `unknown types`.


Where DressCode Runs
--------------------

DressCode needs PHP 8.4 and recent versions of its own libraries, including `nette/utils` 4.1. That is why it cannot be a development dependency of an application that is still on an older PHP or on Nette 3. Install it globally, together with PHPStan and the upgrade package of your ecosystem:

```shell
composer global require dresscode/dresscode dresscode/upgrade-nette phpstan/phpstan
```

Instead of `upgrade-nette`, use `upgrade-symfony`, `upgrade-laravel`, or `upgrade-deegee` for Dibi and Texy; the [table of packages |upgrading-libraries#Packages for Ecosystems] shows what each one covers. A project that is already on the new versions and only wants to watch for the old API can also have DressCode as a development dependency, see [below |#Keeping the Old API from Coming Back].


The Upgrade Plan
================

The command `upgrade plan` prints the estimate followed by the plan, which is the estimate arranged into steps; the option `--save` also saves the plan. The estimate is the same as above, so the output below replaces it with `...`:

```shell
dresscode upgrade plan nette/utils 4.0 --save
```

/--pre .[terminal]
DRESS|CODE 1.3.0
Config     /var/www/shop/dresscode.neon
Upgrade    nette/utils 4.0 over 3 files
...
Mode       application, `composer.json` gives the type `project`
Plan       nette/utils 4.0
  1  fix       works on the versions allowed today  nette/utils
  2  composer  raise nette/utils to ^4.0
  3  fix       needs the targets                    nette/utils
  4  composer  update to the newest versions
Tests      not run, no `--test-command` given
Saved      dresscode-upgrade.neon
Next       `dresscode upgrade run` carries out the plan saved, step by step
\--

The plan goes from the rewrites that already work today (the bridge), through raising the constraint in `composer.json`, to the rewrites for the new version (the jump). After them would come a cleanup of code for the versions the project has left behind, such as the condition `PHP_VERSION_ID < 80100`, which is never true after the move to PHP 8.4, and finally a skipped step with the set `modernizations`, that is, with what newer PHP offers. Here both are missing, because there is nothing for them to do. The plan of an application ends with a step that updates the packages to the newest versions the constraints allow; the line `Mode` says that the project is an application, and why. A library is planned differently, see [below |#A Library Widens, It Does Not Raise].

A step that cannot be done yet is preceded by a manual step, marked `manual` in the plan. When something in the lock refuses the target version, the plan starts with the command that tells you why, such as `composer why-not nette/utils 4.0`. The run stops at such a step with exit code 4 and moves past it once Composer reports that nothing in the lock refuses the target version any more. Without a `composer.lock` there is nothing to ask Composer about, so the run waits the same way; if you know that nothing blocks the step, skip it with `skip: true`.

The file `dresscode-upgrade.neon` in the root of the project is ordinary NEON and belongs in git: the team reviews it, leaves a step out by writing `skip: true`, narrows a topic or adds the test command. DressCode reads the file strictly: an unknown key or a value of the wrong type ends in an error that says where in the file it is, so a typo never silently changes a run. If you narrowed the estimate with `--paths`, the plan keeps those paths and its fixes stay within them. A step is a recipe, not a result: it says which topics to fix and how to raise the constraints, not how many places change; DressCode counts those again when it reaches the step:

```neon
version: 1

targets:
	nette/utils: '4.0'

steps:
	-
		title: works on the versions allowed today
		kind: fix
		topics:
			- nette/utils

	-
		title: raise nette/utils to ^4.0
		kind: composer
		require:
			nette/utils: ^4.0

	-
		title: needs the targets
		kind: fix
		topics:
			- nette/utils

	-
		title: update to the newest versions
		kind: update
```

The step `kind: update` updates the packages to the newest versions the constraints allow, and it counts as done once `composer update --dry-run` reports no changes.

The plan records what the estimate could not assess under the key `estimate`: the files it could not process under `unprocessed`, and the missing upgrade packages under `missingData`, each with the libraries it covers. A run of such a plan warns about this right at the start, because its steps may have missed those places, and so it checks the whole code at the end, see [Manual Tasks Along the Way |#Manual Tasks Along the Way].


Carrying Out the Plan
=====================

Given a target, `upgrade run` saves the plan and carries it out; without one, it carries out the saved plan. Each topic is fixed separately, in the style the code follows. This is the output of `dresscode upgrade run` for the plan above:

/--pre .[terminal]
DRESS|CODE 1.3.0
Config     /var/www/shop/dresscode.neon
Step 1     nette/utils 1 file
Waiting    step 2, raise nette/utils to ^4.0: run `composer require "nette/utils:^4.0" -W --prefer-lowest --minimal-changes`, then `dresscode upgrade run`
\--

The bridge is done, and the run stopped at the Composer step, because outside a terminal it asks no questions. In a terminal, it shows what will change in the project and asks whether it may change `composer.json` and update the packages; outside one, it does so only with the option `--yes`. Otherwise it does not even call Composer; it only prints the `composer require` command and ends with exit code 4, which means it is waiting for a step outside DressCode. You can run Composer yourself and then `upgrade run`. With `--yes`, the run continues on its own:

/--pre .[terminal]
DRESS|CODE 1.3.0
Config     /var/www/shop/dresscode.neon
Warning    The working tree has changes of its own, which the changes of the upgrade mix with.
Composer   raise nette/utils to ^4.0, which changes:
  - Upgrading nette/utils (v3.2.10 => v4.0.0)
Composer   update to the newest versions, which changes:
  - Upgrading nette/application (v3.1.15 => v3.3.0)
  - Upgrading nette/component-model (v3.0.3 => v4.0.2)
  - Upgrading nette/http (v3.2.4 => v3.4.2)
  - Upgrading nette/routing (v3.0.5 => v3.1.5)
  - Upgrading nette/utils (v4.0.0 => v4.1.6)
Step 1     nette/utils left nothing to fix
Step 3     nette/utils 3 files
UPGRADED  4 steps done, and the final check finds nothing more to upgrade, no tests run
\--

The warning appears because the changes from step 1 are not committed yet, so they would mix with those of the jump; commit after each stop and the warning goes away. Composer installs the lowest versions the new constraints allow (`--prefer-lowest --minimal-changes`), because the code is written for them and DressCode reads their `@deprecated` annotations. The other packages change only as much as the update requires, and before you agree, DressCode lists separately any package the raise would move back to a lower version than the lock has. A step that raises PHP also raises `config.platform.php`, if the project sets it. The newest versions come only with the last step of the plan, `update to the newest versions`, which is tested like any other; if you leave it out with `skip: true`, the last line of the run reminds you that the lowest versions are still installed. The line `UPGRADED` says how many steps were done and how many the plan skipped, and that the final check of the whole code found nothing more, see [below |#Manual Tasks Along the Way].

This is the result in the code:

```php .[before]
if (!Strings::endsWith($file, '.jpg')) {
	return Image::fromString(Image::EMPTY_GIF);
}

$image->resize(300, 200, Image::FIT | Image::SHRINK_ONLY);
file_put_contents($this->file, Json::encode($products, Json::PRETTY));
```

```php .[after]
if (!\str_ends_with($file, '.jpg')) {
	return Image::fromString(Image::EmptyGIF);
}

$image->resize(300, 200, Image::OrSmaller | Image::ShrinkOnly);
file_put_contents($this->file, Json::encode($products, pretty: true));
```

Notice that step 1 fixed nothing in the second run. DressCode works out where the plan stopped from the code and the lock, not from what a run stores for going back: a topic with nothing left to fix is done, and a Composer step is done when `composer.json` already has its constraint and the installed version is one it allows. So `upgrade run` continues correctly even after the team has squashed or reordered the commits, or after you ran Composer yourself.

After a raise through Composer, the run continues in a new DressCode process, so that the fixes for the new version, the tests and the check at the end of the plan see what the raise installed, including plugins, libraries and upgrade data. For you, it is still one run with one output. The only exception is DressCode running inside another program that reads its output: it starts no new process, stops after the raise with exit code 4 and tells you to run `dresscode upgrade run` again.


Tests and Commits
-----------------

DressCode runs your tests only when you tell it how. `--test-command` is the test command of your project, in which `{junit}` stands for a JUnit file the tests write their results to; both Nette Tester and PHPUnit can write this format:

```shell
dresscode upgrade run nette/utils 4.0 --test-command "vendor/bin/tester tests -o junit:{junit}"
```

Before the first step, DressCode runs the tests and remembers which ones passed. If a test that used to pass fails, is skipped or disappears after a step, DressCode restores the files of the step byte for byte, except for any file you changed in the meantime, and ends with exit code 1. The dependencies Composer raised stay raised, though; the output then names `composer.json` and the lock on the line `Raised`, and `dresscode upgrade abort` puts them back, see [below |#Interrupted Runs and Going Back]. A test run that writes no valid JUnit report, or does not finish in time, also counts as a failure, never as a pass. A quicker subset of tests for the steps where little happens goes into `--quick-test-command`, and its results are compared with its own results from before the first step; the whole suite runs after the jump and after the last step. The `Tests` line of the plan already says which command runs after which step. If the tests cannot run even before the first step, the run ends before changing anything, with exit code 1 and the reason. The `Tests` line of the run then says what the tests were compared with: when they ran before the first step and how many of them failed even then, because having no new failures is not the same as having all tests pass. A command without `{junit}` is refused as soon as the plan is made, and also in a plan file edited by hand.

Commits are up to you. Start with a clean working tree, so that the changes of the upgrade do not mix with unfinished work; if it is not clean, DressCode tells you. With the option `--commit`, it makes one commit per topic, but only once the tests of the step pass. It builds each commit from the file contents the topic produced, using a git index of its own, so it never rewrites the working tree to make a commit, and the hooks of the repository run as they do for any other commit. The raise through Composer gets a commit of its own too, with `composer.json` and the lock unless git ignores them, so that the next run does not mistake those files for unfinished work. A commit takes whole files, so `--commit` is refused before anything changes if the tracked files already have changes of their own, or if git finds no repository tracking the project.

If someone else changes a file after the step wrote it, whether it is the tests or you, the commit would contain code the tests never checked. So the step stays uncommitted in the working tree, and the run asks you to commit it yourself. If git refuses a commit, for example because of a hook, the run stops with exit code 2 and says what is left uncommitted and which topics of the step it had already committed. A step that left files it could not process stays uncommitted until a later run processes them and commits the whole step. If you edit the plan in the meantime so that the step with that number does something else, the run refuses to continue: commit the changes of the step yourself, and `upgrade run --discard` makes the run forget the step.


Manual Tasks Along the Way
--------------------------

The run lists what the fixes leave behind as manual tasks under the steps (the line `Manual`), each with the place and a message saying what to write instead. While any task remains, the run stops after the jump and at the end of the plan, with exit code 1; the plan is done only once none is left. Fix each place, or confirm it with a comment that gives the reason, and run `upgrade run` again:

```php
// dresscode:ignore upgrading.php.deprecatedApi -- the report needs the locale of the server
$month = strftime('%B');
```

A confirming comment shows up in the diff, so a colleague sees it during review too. A place you want to keep for good can also go into the [baseline |suppressions-and-baseline].

`dresscode upgrade tasks` lists the manual tasks of the saved plan that are still left in the code, including those found only by the check of the whole code at the end of the plan, and changes nothing. Under `Exceptions`, it adds the `dresscode:ignore` comments that give no reason: they only postpone a task and do not count as accepted exceptions yet. Under `Unverified`, it lists the files the fixes could not process. For a risky fix, it says what the fix is waiting for and how to allow it. It ends with exit code 1 while any task or such comment remains, and with 2 when a file was left unprocessed, so it works in CI too.

At the end of the plan, DressCode checks the whole code once more with the set `deprecations`, not only the topics the estimate found. This is the first point where a library installed only by the raise can show what it deprecates. If the check finds anything, the run ends with exit code 1 and lists it: files that `dresscode upgrade today` will fix, or manual tasks. Then run `upgrade run` again. A plan completed without some of the upgrade data says so on its last line.

A file the fixes cannot process, for example one that does not parse, is listed under `Unverified`, and the run stops after its step with exit code 2, because a file left unprocessed makes the result incomplete.


Interrupted Runs and Going Back
-------------------------------

If you interrupt a run in the middle of a step, some files may already have been rewritten. The next run therefore lists them and does nothing else: put them back with `dresscode upgrade abort`, or continue from the code as it is with `upgrade run --discard`. `upgrade plan --save` stops the same way, because a new plan would forget the unfinished step.

`dresscode upgrade abort` puts back everything a run stores for going back: the files of an unfinished step, and `composer.json` and `composer.lock` as they were before the first raise through Composer, even when they are located above the configuration directory. The run keeps the Composer files until a step after the raise is done. Whenever it stops with the Composer files changed, for example when the tests fail after the raise or Composer fails halfway through, it lists them on the line `Raised`. When Composer fails, the run shows the last line of its output and the path to a log in the temporary directory with the full output. After putting the files back, `abort` runs `composer install`, so that the packages in vendor match the restored lock. It leaves alone any file someone has changed since and lists it on the line `Left`; in that case, and also when `composer install` fails, it ends with exit code 1. `upgrade abort --dry-run` tells you in advance what it would put back and what it would leave, without changing anything.

`dresscode upgrade status` tells you where the saved plan stands, without running a single fix. For a Composer step, it reads from the constraints whether the step is done or due; for a manual step, it reads from the lock whether the lock still refuses the target. It does not count how much is left to fix; that is up to `upgrade run`. Below the plan, it lists what a run has stored: an unfinished step, a raise that `abort` would put back, a step left uncommitted, and when the tests ran before the first step. It always ends with exit code 0:

/--pre .[terminal]
DRESS|CODE 1.3.0
Config     /var/www/shop/dresscode.neon
Plan       nette/utils 4.0
  1  works on the versions allowed today  what is left `upgrade run` fixes and `upgrade tasks` lists
  2  raise nette/utils to ^4.0            done
  3  needs the targets                    what is left `upgrade run` fixes and `upgrade tasks` lists
  4  update to the newest versions        `upgrade run` tells
Raised     step 2 changed composer.json, composer.lock, which `dresscode upgrade abort` puts back
Next       `dresscode upgrade run` carries on from where the code stands
\--

A run stores this in the project's git repository, in `.git/dresscode-upgrade/`, so clearing the cache does not lose it. A project without a repository keeps it in the cache directory, and the run warns about it. Two runs never work on one project at once: the second is refused until the first one ends.


A Library Widens, It Does Not Raise
-----------------------------------

A library, a project with `"type": "library"` and no `composer.lock` of its own, does not raise versions: its code must keep working with the older ones it supports today. So its plan contains only the bridge and the constraint widened to include the target, from `^3.2` to `^3.2 || ^4.0`, written with `--no-update`. Whatever would be rewritten only after the jump is reported for now, see [below |#Lower Bounds Decide]. For a project whose `type` says library but which has a lock of its own, DressCode asks in a terminal and follows the `type` outside one; the options `--library` and `--no-library` settle it up front.


What Is Left to You
===================

An upgrade is a rewrite plus a list of what is left, and that list is half of its value. DressCode does not guess: where there is no replacement, or a human has to choose it, it reports the place and says what to write instead:

```
Constant `Nette\Application\IRouter::SECURED` is forbidden: write the protocol, `https://`, into the mask of the route.
Method `Nette\Http\UrlScript::setPath()` is forbidden: the class is immutable; take a changed copy with `withPath()`.
```

Each such message is an instruction, so you can go through the list yourself or hand it to an AI agent as it is. `upgrade today -f agent` gives the agent the list in a form written for a model, and `--format json` outputs the estimate, the plan, the run and `upgrade tasks` as data, see [AI agents |ai-agents#Upgrading with an Agent]. DressCode also reports places where the data offers a fix that cannot be applied there, for example because it would throw away the expression the method is called on, or because a comment sits between the arguments. The message gives the reason, and the code stays as it was.

**Risky fixes** wait for your permission. A few rewrites could change what the code does, for example reading a property on the left of `??`, where PHP first checks whether it is set. In the plan, such a fix is a manual task. The decisions you trust for good go into the key [`fixRisky` |configuration#risky-fixes], and `dresscode upgrade today --ask-risky` goes through them with you one by one, showing the diff of each.

**What the data does not see.** The data does not capture changes in behavior, configuration in NEON or the syntax of templates; the upgrade guides of the individual libraries describe those. And when a library removes something that the data does not record, only PHPStan will find it.


Lower Bounds Decide
===================

The version the code is rewritten for is decided neither by the version installed in vendor nor by the PHP DressCode happens to run on, but by the constraints in `composer.json`. The code must work with every version a constraint allows, so it is written for the lowest one: code written for a newer version would break wherever the constraint lets in an older one. With `"nette/forms": "^3.2"`, the code is therefore rewritten to the API of version 3.2, even if 3.3 is installed in vendor. DressCode reads the PHP version the same way, from `require.php`. Composer does not raise the lower bound of PHP by itself, even when the project runs on a newer PHP; the key `config.platform.php` does: with `^8.1` and the platform `8.3`, the code is written for PHP 8.3.

So upgrading does not mean installing a newer version, but raising the lower bound. That is why the plan raises the constraints in `composer.json` first and only then rewrites the code for the new version.

Changes in higher versions that the constraint also allows are not rewritten but reported, because the code will run on them one day. With `"^3.2 || ^4.0"`, the code is rewritten to the API of version 3.2, and what 4.0 changed is reported together with the version:

```
Class `Acme\Old` is deprecated: replaced by `Acme\New`, since acme/lib 4.0.
```

The word `deprecated` says that the installed version still has the thing; `removed` says that it no longer does. A library that the project requires without a lower bound (`*`), or does not require at all and gets only as a dependency of another library, can be installed in any version. So its data rewrites nothing; everything is only reported. The header of the `upgrade today` output lists such libraries on the line `Reported`, and on the line `Lowest` the libraries installed in a higher version than the lowest one their constraint allows.

`dresscode config` shows where the data starts and where raising the lower bound would lead. Versions marked `(reported)` are already allowed by the constraint, so their changes are only reported:

/--pre .[terminal]
Packages   3 with upgrading data
      nette/application 3.2           upgrading/application.neon of dresscode/upgrade-nette, upgrading further to 3.2.1 (reported), 3.2.3 (reported), 3.2.9 (reported), 3.2.10 (reported), 3.3 (reported)
      nette/component-model 3.2       upgrading/component-model.neon of dresscode/upgrade-nette, upgrading further to 4.0
      nette/utils 4.1                 upgrading/utils.neon of dresscode/upgrade-nette, upgrading further to 4.1.5 (reported)
\--


When You Have Already Raised the Versions
=========================================

The plan does everything at once. If you have already raised the constraints in `composer.json` yourself, for example with `composer require`, and only want to rewrite the code for the versions the project now requires, run one command:

```shell
dresscode upgrade today
```

What `dresscode upgrade today` does:

- **It needs no configuration.** It takes the whole project directory, and the versions of the libraries and of PHP from the constraints in `composer.json`, see [above |#Lower Bounds Decide].
- **It rewrites only the old API.** It runs the set [`deprecations` |php-coding-standards#deprecations]: the data of the installed upgrade packages with their rules, plus the data about what PHP has deprecated. With the option `--modernize`, it also rewrites the code to newer PHP constructs (the set `modernizations`).
- **It does not touch the style.** Without a configuration it has no style; with a configuration, it takes from it only what the code already follows, see [below |#Your Style Stays].
- **It asks PHPStan about types**, because without them it cannot tell what object a method is called on. If PHPStan is not installed, it stops with an error.
- **It tells you what is left**, and if it rewrote something, it advises you to run it again.

This is what an upgrade of a Nette application looks like:

/--pre .[terminal]
DRESS|CODE 1.3.0
Config     /var/www/shop/dresscode.neon
Target     PHP 8.2 from `composer.json`
Upgrading  nette/application, nette/component-model, nette/forms, nette/http, nette/utils
Fixing     214 files in /var/www/shop

app/Presentation/Product/ProductPresenter.php  rewritten
app/Presentation/Sign/SignPresenter.php  rewritten
app/Model/Referrals.php
  error  27:26  Method `Nette\Http\Request::getReferer()` is forbidden: use `getOrigin()`, which reads the Origin header and gives only the scheme, the host and the port instead of the whole URL of the referring page.  upgrading.api.forbiddenMembers

FIXED  18 violations found, 1 remaining in 3 of 214 files, 3.2 s
Run `dresscode upgrade today` again: the code it rewrote may hold more to upgrade.
\--

The line `Upgrading` names the libraries whose data applies. DressCode rewrote the files marked `rewritten`; under each of the other files, the output lists what is left to do. The last line advises a second run, because the rewritten code may contain something else to upgrade that the first run could not see yet. A run that rewrites nothing does not print this advice. `upgrade today` also works in [continuous integration |continuous-integration]: with `--max-warnings 0`, it ends with exit code 1 if even one warning is left.


A Diff You Can Trust
--------------------

`upgrade today` writes the changes right away, and git shows them. So start with a clean working tree and read the diff after the run. You will find only the places where the old API was used, rewritten in the style of the surrounding code, imports included. DressCode does not search and replace text. It rewrites a method call only where the types say the method belongs to that class: `getName()` is rewritten where it is called on `FileUpload` and nowhere else. [How Upgrading Libraries Works |upgrading-libraries] explains the mechanism and how DressCode knows what has changed. Want to start carefully? Run it on one file first, `dresscode upgrade today --paths app/Model/Orders.php`, and look at what it did.

Read the diff anyway. The data describes how code is written, not what it means: when a new version returns `-1` instead of `null`, DressCode rewrites the name of the method but leaves the condition `=== null` as it is. So after the upgrade, run your tests and PHPStan.


Your Style Stays
----------------

A project without `dresscode.neon` has no style, and `upgrade today` changes only the old API; the step-by-step plan needs a configuration, and without one it points you to `dresscode init`. If the project has a configuration, everything in it that concerns the upgrade applies: `paths` and `excludePaths`, the target PHP version, `fixRisky`, the baseline, the overrides in `overrides`, and your own [replacement maps |upgrading-libraries#Your Own Renames in a Project]. From the style, only the parts the code already follows apply: DressCode first checks the code against the style of the project and leaves out every decision the code violates somewhere. So new code takes the form the project really uses, and a file the upgrade touches is not reformatted.

The unit is the decision, and the choice holds for the whole project: a single long line anywhere leaves out `file.longLines` everywhere. If you want the style as well, run `dresscode fix` afterwards, ideally as a separate commit.


Missing Data
------------

`upgrade` learns from Packagist which upgrade packages exist, for now only those of the vendor `dresscode`. It asks at most once a day and remembers the answer in the cache directory, so it also works offline; with no network and no remembered answer, it simply offers nothing. The estimate names a missing package, and `upgrade today` offers to install it, but only when the project itself requires one of its libraries in `require` or `require-dev`, not when the library came only as a dependency of another one:

/--pre .[terminal]
The libraries of the project have upgrading data in `dresscode/upgrade-nette`, which DressCode does not have.
Install it beside DressCode in `C:/Users/me/AppData/Roaming/Composer`?
\--

The package goes into the DressCode installation, so the `composer.json` of the project stays unchanged. If DressCode is a dependency of the project (in its `composer.lock`), the package is installed into the project with `composer require --dev`, and the question warns that the `composer.json` of the project will change. Without a terminal, in CI or in an agent hook, `upgrade today` installs nothing: it ends with exit code 3 and prints the exact Composer command. The option `--yes` allows the installation even there.


One Major Version at a Time, by Hand
------------------------------------

Without a plan, split a big jump into steps, one major version of a library at a time. Each step is the same:

1. In `composer.json`, raise the lower bound to the next major version, for example from `"nette/utils": "^3.2"` to `"^4.0"`. If you are also moving to a newer PHP, raise `require.php`, and `config.platform.php` too if the project sets it.
2. Run `composer update --prefer-lowest`, so that vendor holds exactly the lowest versions the code is written for.
3. Run `dresscode upgrade today`, and if it advises you to, run it once more.
4. Go through what is left, run the tests and PHPStan, read the diff and commit.
5. Continue with the next version, or bring the newest versions back into vendor with a plain `composer update`.

Why `--prefer-lowest`? Where there is no data, DressCode rewrites according to the `@deprecated` annotations in the code of the library. But an annotation speaks only for the installed version. So DressCode ignores the annotations of a library installed in a higher version than the lowest one the constraint allows: the replacement might not exist in the older version.

Why in steps? Because in each step, the data meets exactly the version of the library it describes. The new classes the data writes into your code are already in vendor, and the `@deprecated` annotations of the installed version reveal what will disappear in the next one; DressCode uses them to rewrite the code right away, or at least to show where you will need to change it. [How Upgrading Libraries Works |upgrading-libraries#One Version at a Time, or All at Once] describes how to upgrade an application all at once, or how to rewrite the code before you update the library.


Keeping the Old API from Coming Back
====================================

An upgrade is a one-time cleanup. To make every check in [continuous integration |continuous-integration] and in a [Git hook |git-hooks] watch for the old API too, add the set `deprecations` and the types to the configuration, and name the package in `use` to enable its rules as well:

```neon
typeAnalysis: phpstan

use:
	- perCs           # your standard
	- deprecations
	- upgrade-nette
```

Packages of the vendor `dresscode` can be written short in `use`, like built-in sets; the full name `dresscode/upgrade-nette` works too. The data of a package applies even when `use` does not name it; the name adds the rules the package carries alongside the data (for Nette, the conversion of the flag `Json::PRETTY` to a named argument). If the name is missing, `check` and `fix` point it out once:

/--pre .[terminal]
Warning: Package `dresscode/upgrade-nette` brings rules that run only where `use` names it, as `upgrade-nette`, and in `dresscode upgrade`; skipped, with what its upgrading files set for them.
\--

From then on, `dresscode check` reports the old API in every new piece of code. And because the data follows the constraints in `composer.json`, the check itself reports what needs rewriting as soon as you raise a constraint to the next version.


Upgrading a Library You Maintain
================================

A library that depends on other libraries is upgraded the same way as an application, except that its constraints are widened instead of raised, see [above |#A Library Widens, It Does Not Raise]. When I upgraded `nette/application` like this, `upgrade today` rewrote `setFactory()` to `setCreator()` in the code and in the tests, and the flag `RouteList::ONE_WAY` to the named argument `oneWay: 1`. It reported a deprecated property and an annotation whose text does not fit into an attribute, and the second run rewrote nothing more.

But a library also has tests that use the old API on purpose: a test of a deprecated attribute, a test of backward compatibility, a type test of an interface. You do not want those rewritten. Exclude such a file from the data with an [override |configuration#Different Values for Part of the Project] in `overrides`:

```neon
typeAnalysis: phpstan

use:
	- nette
	- deprecations

overrides:
	-
		paths: [tests/UI/Requires.sameSite.handle.phpt]
		upgrading:
			packages: no
			declarations:
				deprecatedMember: keep
```

`packages: no` turns off the data of the packages for that file, together with the rewrites their files turn on, and `deprecatedMember: keep` turns off rewriting by `@deprecated` annotations; the style and the rest of the set still apply. For a single line, the comment `// dresscode:ignore upgrading` is enough, see [Suppressions |suppressions-and-baseline]. What `upgrade` still reports in such tests is expected: it is old API that is there on purpose.

The data does not rewrite the code of the class itself, because it describes how the class is used, not how it is implemented. When the data of `nette/application` says that a write to `$request->post` should become `setPost($value)`, the line `$this->post = $post` inside the method `Request::setPost()` stays as it is; otherwise the method would call itself. A child class, though, is a user like any other and gets upgraded. Data about a magic property, that is, about `__get()` and `__set()`, also applies to reading and writing a private property from outside, because PHP routes those to `__get()` and `__set()` as well.

Want to upgrade in parts, for example Nette first and PHP later? Give the estimate and the plan a single target, or narrow `upgrade today` with the option `--only` to the data of one package and the rules that read it:

```shell
dresscode upgrade today --only upgrade-nette
```

The data of the other packages takes part only where another name in `--only` covers it, for example `deprecations` or `upgrading.api`. You can narrow it further with the name of a library: `--only nette/forms` takes only the files about `nette/forms`, whichever package carries them, and `--only php` the data about what PHP has deprecated. `check` and `fix` support the same `--only`, and the keys `fixRisky` and `severity` also accept the name of a package or a library. Naming a package whose data has no effect here is an error, and the message says why: the data does not cover any version of the project's libraries, or `upgrading.packages` is not `yes`. A name that is both an upgrade package and a library another package writes about stands for both: the files of that package and the files about that library.


